\"get\";a:0:{}s:4:\"post\";a:3:{s:8:\"model_id\";s:16:\"gemini-2.0-flash\";s:15:\"generation_type\";s:4:\"text\";s:6:\"prompt\";s:22:\"<p>sample php code</p>\";}}result|s:5052:\"<p>PHP (Hypertext Preprocessor) is a widely-used open source general-purpose scripting language that is especially suited for web development and can be embedded into HTML. Here are some sample PHP code snippets that demonstrate common functionalities.</p>\n<h3>1. Hello, World!</h3>\n<p>This is the most basic PHP script, which outputs a simple text string.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-php\">&lt;?php\n  echo \"Hello, World!\";\n?&gt;</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>&lt;?php</code> and <code>?&gt;</code> are the opening and closing tags that mark the beginning and end of PHP code.</li>\n<li><code>echo</code> is a language construct that outputs data to the browser.</li>\n<li>The text &quot;Hello, World!&quot; is the string that will be displayed.</li>\n<li>Every PHP statement must end with a semicolon (<code>;</code>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. Working with Variables and Arrays</h3>\n<p>This example demonstrates how to declare variables and work with an associative array.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-php\">&lt;?php\n  // Declaring variables\n  $name = \"John Doe\";\n  $age = 30;\n\n  // Creating an associative array\n  $person = array(\n      \"name\" =&gt; \"Jane Doe\",\n      \"age\" =&gt; 25,\n      \"city\" =&gt; \"New York\"\n  );\n\n  // Accessing and displaying information\n  echo \"Name: \" . $name . \"&lt;br&gt;\";\n  echo \"Age: \" . $age . \"&lt;br&gt;\";\n\n  echo \"&lt;h2&gt;Person Details&lt;/h2&gt;\";\n  echo \"Name: \" . $person[\"name\"] . \"&lt;br&gt;\";\n  echo \"Age: \" . $person[\"age\"] . \"&lt;br&gt;\";\n  echo \"City: \" . $person[\"city\"];\n?&gt;</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Variables in PHP start with a <code>$</code> sign.</li>\n<li>The <code>.</code> is used for string concatenation.</li>\n<li><code>&lt;br&gt;</code> is an HTML tag for a line break.</li>\n<li>An associative array uses named keys that you assign to them.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>3. Simple Calculator using Functions</h3>\n<p>This snippet shows how to define and use functions to perform calculations.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-php\">&lt;?php\n  // Function to add two numbers\n  function add($a, $b) {\n    return $a + $b;\n  }\n\n  // Function to subtract two numbers\n  function subtract($a, $b) {\n    return $a - $b;\n  }\n\n  $num1 = 20;\n  $num2 = 10;\n\n  echo \"Sum: \" . add($num1, $num2) . \"&lt;br&gt;\";\n  echo \"Difference: \" . subtract($num1, $num2);\n?&gt;</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <code>function</code> keyword is used to create a new function.</li>\n<li>Functions can take arguments (in this case, <code>$a</code> and <code>$b</code>) and return a value using the <code>return</code> statement.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>4. Handling Form Submissions</h3>\n<p>A common use of PHP is to process data submitted from an HTML form.</p>\n<p><strong>HTML Form (index.html):</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n\n&lt;form action=\"welcome.php\" method=\"post\"&gt;\nName: &lt;input type=\"text\" name=\"name\"&gt;&lt;br&gt;\nE-mail: &lt;input type=\"text\" name=\"email\"&gt;&lt;br&gt;\n&lt;input type=\"submit\"&gt;\n&lt;/form&gt;\n\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>\n<p><strong>PHP Script (welcome.php):</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-php\">&lt;?php\n  if ($_SERVER[\"REQUEST_METHOD\"] == \"POST\") {\n    $name = htmlspecialchars($_POST[\'name\']);\n    $email = htmlspecialchars($_POST[\'email\']);\n    echo \"Welcome, \" . $name . \"!&lt;br&gt;\";\n    echo \"Your email address is: \" . $email;\n  }\n?&gt;</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The HTML form sends data to <code>welcome.php</code> using the <code>POST</code> method.</li>\n<li><code>$_SERVER[\"REQUEST_METHOD\"]</code> is a superglobal variable that returns the request method used to access the page.</li>\n<li><code>$_POST</code> is another superglobal variable that collects data from the HTML form.</li>\n<li><code>htmlspecialchars()</code> is a security function that converts special characters to HTML entities to prevent cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>5. Connecting to a MySQL Database</h3>\n<p>PHP is frequently used to interact with databases. Here\'s a basic example of connecting to a MySQL database using PDO (PHP Data Objects).</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-php\">&lt;?php\n  $servername = \"localhost\";\n  $username = \"username\";\n  $password = \"password\";\n  $dbname = \"myDB\";\n\n  try {\n    $conn = new PDO(\"mysql:host=$servername;dbname=$dbname\", $username, $password);\n    // set the PDO error mode to exception\n    $conn-&gt;setAttribute(PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE, PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION);\n    echo \"Connected successfully\";\n  } catch(PDOException $e) {\n    echo \"Connection failed: \" . $e-&gt;getMessage();\n  }\n?&gt;</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>This script uses PDO to establish a connection to a MySQL database.</li>\n<li>A <code>try...catch</code> block is used to handle potential connection errors gracefully.</li>\n<li>If the connection is successful, a success message is displayed; otherwise, the error is caught and an error message is shown.</li>\n</ul>\";raw_result|s:4148:\"PHP (Hypertext Preprocessor) is a widely-used open source general-purpose scripting language that is especially suited for web development and can be embedded into HTML. Here are some sample PHP code snippets that demonstrate common functionalities.\n\n### 1. Hello, World!\n\nThis is the most basic PHP script, which outputs a simple text string.\n\n```php\n<?php\n  echo \"Hello, World!\";\n?>\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n*   `<?php` and `?>` are the opening and closing tags that mark the beginning and end of PHP code.\n*   `echo` is a language construct that outputs data to the browser.\n*   The text \"Hello, World!\" is the string that will be displayed.\n*   Every PHP statement must end with a semicolon (`;`).\n\n### 2. Working with Variables and Arrays\n\nThis example demonstrates how to declare variables and work with an associative array.\n\n```php\n<?php\n  // Declaring variables\n  $name = \"John Doe\";\n  $age = 30;\n\n  // Creating an associative array\n  $person = array(\n      \"name\" => \"Jane Doe\",\n      \"age\" => 25,\n      \"city\" => \"New York\"\n  );\n\n  // Accessing and displaying information\n  echo \"Name: \" . $name . \"<br>\";\n  echo \"Age: \" . $age . \"<br>\";\n\n  echo \"<h2>Person Details</h2>\";\n  echo \"Name: \" . $person[\"name\"] . \"<br>\";\n  echo \"Age: \" . $person[\"age\"] . \"<br>\";\n  echo \"City: \" . $person[\"city\"];\n?>\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n*   Variables in PHP start with a `$` sign.\n*   The `.` is used for string concatenation.\n*   `<br>` is an HTML tag for a line break.\n*   An associative array uses named keys that you assign to them.\n\n### 3. Simple Calculator using Functions\n\nThis snippet shows how to define and use functions to perform calculations.\n\n```php\n<?php\n  // Function to add two numbers\n  function add($a, $b) {\n    return $a + $b;\n  }\n\n  // Function to subtract two numbers\n  function subtract($a, $b) {\n    return $a - $b;\n  }\n\n  $num1 = 20;\n  $num2 = 10;\n\n  echo \"Sum: \" . add($num1, $num2) . \"<br>\";\n  echo \"Difference: \" . subtract($num1, $num2);\n?>\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n*   The `function` keyword is used to create a new function.\n*   Functions can take arguments (in this case, `$a` and `$b`) and return a value using the `return` statement.\n\n### 4. Handling Form Submissions\n\nA common use of PHP is to process data submitted from an HTML form.\n\n**HTML Form (index.html):**\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<body>\n\n<form action=\"welcome.php\" method=\"post\">\nName: <input type=\"text\" name=\"name\"><br>\nE-mail: <input type=\"text\" name=\"email\"><br>\n<input type=\"submit\">\n</form>\n\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n\n**PHP Script (welcome.php):**\n```php\n<?php\n  if ($_SERVER[\"REQUEST_METHOD\"] == \"POST\") {\n    $name = htmlspecialchars($_POST[\'name\']);\n    $email = htmlspecialchars($_POST[\'email\']);\n    echo \"Welcome, \" . $name . \"!<br>\";\n    echo \"Your email address is: \" . $email;\n  }\n?>\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n*   The HTML form sends data to `welcome.php` using the `POST` method.\n*   `$_SERVER[\"REQUEST_METHOD\"]` is a superglobal variable that returns the request method used to access the page.\n*   `$_POST` is another superglobal variable that collects data from the HTML form.\n*   `htmlspecialchars()` is a security function that converts special characters to HTML entities to prevent cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks.\n\n### 5. Connecting to a MySQL Database\n\nPHP is frequently used to interact with databases. Here\'s a basic example of connecting to a MySQL database using PDO (PHP Data Objects).\n\n```php\n<?php\n  $servername = \"localhost\";\n  $username = \"username\";\n  $password = \"password\";\n  $dbname = \"myDB\";\n\n  try {\n    $conn = new PDO(\"mysql:host=$servername;dbname=$dbname\", $username, $password);\n    // set the PDO error mode to exception\n    $conn->setAttribute(PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE, PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION);\n    echo \"Connected successfully\";\n  } catch(PDOException $e) {\n    echo \"Connection failed: \" . $e->getMessage();\n  }\n?>\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n*   This script uses PDO to establish a connection to a MySQL database.\n*   A `try...catch` block is used to handle potential connection errors gracefully.\n*   If the connection is successful, a success message is displayed; otherwise, the error is caught and an error message is shown.\";'),('ci_session:1a781e2e9456f4872ee9fd9412e12d3b','::1',4294967295,_binary '__ci_last_regenerate|i:1764601384;_ci_previous_url|s:24:\"htt